How the string methods are shared. `methods.hpp` defines `sv::impl::methods<T, C>`, a CRTP
base that writes every read-only text method once, for any type T with `view() -> sv::basic<C>`:
the views themselves, `ca::basic_str` and `ca::inline_str` all derive it, so a method added here
appears on every string type at once. The methods return views into the text and never
allocate.

`classify.hpp` holds the per-code-point helpers the methods walk with: `point_at` and
`point_before` decode one code point forwards or backwards (ASCII without decoding), and
`space_at`, `space_before` and `line_break_at` measure a white space code point or a line
boundary, 0 when there is none. Walking backwards meets the same code points as walking
forwards, so `strip_end` and `split` agree on invalid text.

`forward.hpp` declares `sv::basic` ahead of its definition: the methods use `ca::unicode`,
whose functions take views, so the view type must be nameable first.

A type of your own gets the whole text API by deriving the base and providing `view()`:

```c++
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

class token final : public ca::sv::impl::methods<token, char> {
	private:
		ca::sv::str _text;

	public:
		constexpr explicit token(const ca::sv::str text) noexcept
		: _text{text} {
		}

		[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto view(void) const noexcept -> ca::sv::str {
			return _text;
		}
};

static_assert(token{"  if  "}.strip() == ca::sv::str{"if"});
static_assert(token{"while"}.is_identifier());
```
